WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.080
Watch this rally.

00:00:01.160 --> 00:00:05.240
Both paddles play perfectly. Shot one.
Shot two. Shot three. Shot four.

00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:07.640
Shot five. Nobody has made a mistake.

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:11.040
And on the sixth shot, the ball goes
past a paddle that did everything right.

00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:12.080
I want to know why.

00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:17.220
An essay I read says Pong
has no perfect defense.

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:19.780
Just a few fixed laws,
and a game to eleven.

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:22.320
I like that line enough
to wonder when it is true.

00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:25.280
Because a sentence like that
should come with a number attached.

00:00:27.167 --> 00:00:29.827
So I built the smallest
Pong I could. A field.

00:00:29.947 --> 00:00:31.687
Two paddles that only move up and down.

00:00:31.767 --> 00:00:33.927
A ball that bounces off
the top and the bottom.

00:00:34.167 --> 00:00:35.407
And one rule that matters later:

00:00:35.427 --> 00:00:37.807
where the ball hits your
paddle steers where it goes.

00:00:38.227 --> 00:00:40.927
I'm Tanmay Kulkarni,
in for Humanitarians AI.

00:00:41.347 --> 00:00:43.567
This is my model,
not any machine you remember,

00:00:43.727 --> 00:00:46.507
and every number you will see comes
from the settings in the corner.

00:00:47.433 --> 00:00:50.193
The moment the ball leaves a paddle,
its direction is fixed.

00:00:50.413 --> 00:00:52.633
The defender can see
exactly where it is going.

00:00:52.773 --> 00:00:54.573
What the defender does not have is time.

00:00:54.713 --> 00:00:58.353
The ball has to cross the whole field, and
that crossing is all the time there is.

00:00:58.473 --> 00:01:00.353
Keep an eye on the two bars in the corner.

00:01:00.633 --> 00:01:03.393
One is how far the paddle can
travel while the ball crosses.

00:01:03.533 --> 00:01:04.973
The other is how far it must.

00:01:06.033 --> 00:01:09.093
Now put yourself in the attacker's
place. You can aim anywhere.

00:01:09.313 --> 00:01:11.213
Every aim gives a different landing spot,

00:01:11.293 --> 00:01:13.393
and together they cover
the whole height of the field.

00:01:13.753 --> 00:01:15.653
The defender cannot
know the aim in advance,

00:01:15.733 --> 00:01:17.513
so the best place to wait is the middle.

00:01:17.813 --> 00:01:21.153
Then the worst shot is the one that
lands at an edge, half the field away.

00:01:22.233 --> 00:01:24.573
So the whole question
shrinks to one comparison.

00:01:25.053 --> 00:01:26.633
In the time the ball takes to cross,

00:01:26.733 --> 00:01:29.793
can the paddle cover about half the field,
minus the paddle's own reach?

00:01:29.893 --> 00:01:33.013
If it can, a perfect defense
exists. If it can't, none does.

00:01:33.073 --> 00:01:36.093
Here, the paddle needs a little over
a quarter of the ball's sideways speed.

00:01:36.413 --> 00:01:38.333
Keep the form, because
it works on any field:

00:01:38.493 --> 00:01:41.293
the defender's reach in one flight,
against half the free height.

00:01:42.267 --> 00:01:45.927
I replayed the same rally three times,
and touched only the paddle's speed.

00:01:46.147 --> 00:01:48.467
Too slow, and the very
first shot gets past.

00:01:48.787 --> 00:01:51.747
A middle setting holds for five
shots, then breaks on the sixth.

00:01:51.967 --> 00:01:54.927
A fast setting holds for thirteen,
and breaks on the fourteenth.

00:01:55.127 --> 00:01:56.567
Same ball, same rules.

00:01:56.747 --> 00:01:58.227
One number decided the story.

00:02:01.467 --> 00:02:03.227
Here is the part I did not expect.

00:02:03.447 --> 00:02:05.027
The ball speeds up on every shot.

00:02:05.047 --> 00:02:06.267
So the crossing gets shorter,

00:02:06.287 --> 00:02:09.667
and the distance the paddle can cover
in one crossing shrinks with it.

00:02:10.047 --> 00:02:11.907
That bar keeps sinking toward the line.

00:02:12.107 --> 00:02:14.507
In my model, a rally
is not only a contest.

00:02:14.687 --> 00:02:16.207
It has a clock inside it.

00:02:18.533 --> 00:02:22.433
I did not want to trust my own arithmetic,
so I counted. For the middle setting,

00:02:22.513 --> 00:02:26.373
the arithmetic said the ball must speed up
four and a bit times, so the sixth shot.

00:02:26.713 --> 00:02:29.553
A search that tries every
aim broke on the sixth shot.

00:02:29.753 --> 00:02:32.153
For the fast setting, 12.6 speed-ups,

00:02:32.173 --> 00:02:35.633
so the fourteenth shot,
and the search broke on the fourteenth.

00:02:38.267 --> 00:02:39.267
There is a second dial.

00:02:39.287 --> 00:02:41.107
So far the attacker could aim anywhere.

00:02:41.427 --> 00:02:42.787
Now let the hit steer the ball less,

00:02:42.807 --> 00:02:45.987
so its up and down speed is at most
a quarter of its sideways speed.

00:02:46.287 --> 00:02:48.667
The line drops, to about 0.215.

00:02:48.707 --> 00:02:51.007
The arithmetic says the tenth
shot for the middle paddle,

00:02:51.027 --> 00:02:55.027
and the nineteenth for the fast one.
Then the count. The tenth. The nineteenth.

00:02:58.500 --> 00:02:59.860
Now change only the field.

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:03.440
Wider and shorter, 1200 by 500,
with a smaller paddle.

00:03:03.820 --> 00:03:05.540
The line falls to 0.188.

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:07.600
Two paddle speeds, 1.5, and four.

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:10.180
The arithmetic says the seventh
shot, and the twenty-seventh.

00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:13.600
The count found the seventh,
and the twenty-seventh. Same form.

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:14.500
A different field.

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:19.800
Here is the question I could not resist:

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:21.940
which single rule would you
change to break the balance?

00:03:22.100 --> 00:03:23.780
In this model, two rules carry it.

00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:26.540
How fast the paddle moves,
and how much a hit can steer.

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:28.520
And the claim turns out to be conditional.

00:03:28.820 --> 00:03:31.460
A fast enough paddle, against
a ball that never speeds up,

00:03:31.480 --> 00:03:34.800
held for 200 shots,
and the arithmetic says it always will.

00:03:35.060 --> 00:03:37.360
The speeding up is what
makes the sentence true.

00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:43.700
One boundary, because I would rather say
it than have you find it. This is a model.

00:03:44.020 --> 00:03:49.100
No spin, no human reaction time, no paddle
edges, no numbers from any real machine.

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:51.900
It shows when a rule set
can be defended perfectly.

00:03:52.340 --> 00:03:54.940
It does not say how any
particular game was built.

00:03:56.900 --> 00:03:57.620
Your turn.

00:03:57.660 --> 00:04:00.960
A field 1000 wide,
500 tall, a paddle 50 long.

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:05.260
The line is the height, minus the paddle,
over twice the width. Work it out.

00:04:10.754 --> 00:04:12.660
0.225. So at the serve,

00:04:12.700 --> 00:04:15.800
a paddle at a quarter of the ball's
sideways speed has a perfect defense,

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:18.080
and one at a fifth does not.
Last question.

00:04:18.520 --> 00:04:21.180
If you could set one dial,
and only one, where would you put it?

00:04:21.320 --> 00:04:24.200
I'm Tanmay Kulkarni,
in for Humanitarians AI.
